1
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:09,320
pickle

2
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,200
Thank you.

3
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:01,097
Digivolve to Bergemon and was able to
drive out the black gear and destroy it.

4
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:03,737
Then Meramon and the
village went back to normal.

5
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:05,879
We'll just see how long that lasts.

6
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,040
We've passed this place before.

7
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,817
You mean we walked all the
way around the whole planet?

8
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,020
That just can't be.

9
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,180
I can't walk that far, can I?

10
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:24,540
I'm so tired.

11
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:29,000
My feet are hot.

12
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:30,877
Looks like we're taking a break.

13
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:33,057
Well, it's not like we
have some place to be.

14
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:34,077
I guess you're right.

15
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,437
There's no reason to hurry.

16
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:36,377
Check out Izzy.

17
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:38,137
I bet he's trying to email the aliens.

18
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:40,455
Maybe he's asking them to beam him up.

19
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:43,377
Still crashed and the warranty's expired.

20
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:45,097
Hey, Izzy, I know how
to get it to boot up.

21
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:46,917
You just gotta give it a
couple of subtle adjustments.

22
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:47,830
Hey, quit it!

23
00:02:47,913 --> 00:02:49,337
Are your brain cells malfunctioning?

24
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:51,577
Gosh, you're acting like
I'm hurting the dumb thing.

25
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,597
Too bad your brain isn't
as big as your hair.

26
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:57,457
Maybe Izzy doesn't want grimy fingerprints
and dents all over his computer.

27
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:58,370
Huh?

28
00:02:58,780 --> 00:02:59,610
Hmm.

29
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:01,630
What?

30
00:03:01,713 --> 00:03:02,543
Hey, look.

31
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:04,617
Do you guys see that smoke over there?

32
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:05,800
I'll check it out.

33
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:08,580
Hey, Tai, wait for me.

34
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:11,420
The attention span of
a gnat. Uh, whatever.

35
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:14,190
Huh.

36
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:16,680
We got graphics. We got sound.

37
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:18,430
Beautiful.

38
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,720
Up and right. Huh?

39
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:24,180
But it indicates the
battery needs recharging.

40
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:27,100
Ah, that's odd.

41
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:29,477
Hey, everybody, get over here, quick!

42
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:30,390
Huh?

43
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:31,303
We're coming, Tai!

44
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:42,640
Looks like some kind of factory.

45
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:47,413
Let's hope they can manufacture
a way for us to get home.

46
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:52,050
Hmm.

47
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:53,157
I wonder what they make in there.

48
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:56,377
I don't know, but wouldn't it be great
if there's a manufacturer's outlet store?

49
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:58,225
They always have killer deals.

50
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:04,509
Don't ask me how, but there
doesn't seem to be anybody here.

51
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:07,017
There's got to be someone
running the equipment.

52
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:07,930
I don't know.

53
00:04:08,013 --> 00:04:10,704
It appears to be doing
quite well all by itself.

54
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:18,097
Matt, what are the machines making?

55
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,060
You got me.

56
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:21,640
Maybe parts for robots or spaceships.

57
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:25,337
Somebody's got to be moving those
belts, and people got to eat.

58
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:29,949
So is there a cafeteria in this place,
because we can really use a good meal?

59
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:32,790
Hello?

60
00:04:32,873 --> 00:04:34,600
Is anybody here? Wait a minute.

61
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:36,997
Don't go any further in this direction.

62
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:38,757
What's the matter with this direction?

63
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:40,997
It's just as good as any
other direction, isn't it?

64
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:41,910
No, wait.

65
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:43,278
What is it, Biyomon?

66
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:44,420
I'm not sure.

67
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:45,597
I heard something.

68
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:46,510
Yeah?

69
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,430
Ah!

70
00:04:49,513 --> 00:04:50,571
Doesn't sound good to me.

71
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:53,340
I say we go inside and have a look around.

72
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,130
Ah!

73
00:04:58,660 --> 00:04:59,490
Ah!

74
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,850
Prodigious!

75
00:05:01,933 --> 00:05:04,057
Now a battery like that could
run my computer forever.

76
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,846
I wonder if there's a
way to access its power.

77
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:08,630
Hey!

78
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:10,630
Huh?

79
00:05:10,713 --> 00:05:12,718
What do you suppose happened to him?

80
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:15,673
Who knows, but let's see if we can help.

81
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,280
It's just a busted robot.

82
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:18,860
It's not a robot.

83
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:20,420
It's Andromon. What?

84
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:22,247
This big clunk is a Digimon?

85
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:25,317
Yes, and very much advanced. Poor thing.

86
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:29,037
I guess he got caught in the gears,
and that's how he got mangled.

87
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:32,414
Maybe if we work together,
we can pull him out of there.

88
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,117
My mom really doesn't want
me moving any heavy objects.

89
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,580
I get bad knees. Relax. We'll do it.

90
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:39,270
Right. Hey!

91
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:43,777
What are you doing, Izzy?

92
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:45,777
I'm trying to tap into this power source.

93
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:50,566
If I can get this baby to fire up, then
we can use my computer to get some help.

94
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:57,060
I think it's moving.

95
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,488
Hey, something's happening.

96
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:03,759
He's coming loose.

97
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:09,570
Yay!

98
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:15,580
I think he's slowly coming, too.

99
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:18,507
He needs a couple of
whacks for a jump start.

100
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:21,760
I am Andromon.

101
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:25,297
Where's this movie where a robot
came to life and ate everyone?

102
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:26,757
He looks friendly enough.

103
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:30,075
And I'm sure if we're nice
to him, he'll be nice to us.

104
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:34,368
Forget what I said about
him being friendly.

105
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:36,580
I shall punish alien intruder.

106
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:40,877
Andromon is one of the most
powerful Digimon of all.

107
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:44,700
His body is a tireless machine
capable of almost anything.

108
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:47,189
Let's give him all we got.

109
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:49,200
Spiral Christmas!

110
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:59,197
Too bad we didn't catch him on a good day.

111
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:00,110
Watch out.

112
00:07:00,193 --> 00:07:01,177
Maybe it is a good day.

113
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:02,440
This is not good.

114
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:08,400
Well, Tai, what do we do now?

115
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:11,277
Agumon, how about trying
to blast the roof?

116
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:12,190
Mm-hmm.

117
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:28,457
Boy, he's going to have one ugly headache.

118
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:29,370
No doubt.

119
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:31,229
Now let's get out of here.

120
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:41,177
This place would be a lot more interesting
if there was a tour guide to explain it.

121
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:44,378
Ooh, maybe with a little
plaid outfit and a bullhorn?

122
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:45,530
Ah-ha.

123
00:07:45,613 --> 00:07:48,654
I've located an access panel
to the interior operations.

124
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:53,280
Hey, let me see, Izzy.

125
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:58,600
What exactly do you think this is?

126
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:02,060
These markings are binary
annotations of some sort.

127
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:03,338
A computer program.

128
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:08,900
A very complicated program, indeed.

129
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,037
Hey, who turned out the lights?

130
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,061
I break out in hives in the dark.

131
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:22,480
What was that?

132
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,980
I hope it isn't Andromon.

133
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:29,577
It might be a good plan to keep on
moving so we don't have to find out.

134
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:30,778
Well, I'm for that.

135
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:34,277
Or we could just stay here
frozen until the monster gets us.

136
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:35,420
Let's sneak away.

137
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:40,697
Can anyone explain why we're tiptoeing
instead of running for our lives?

138
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:42,320
Intruder sighting.

139
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,180
And Andromaw doesn't like intruders.

140
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:48,380
Lightning blade!

141
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:50,330
Hurrah!

142
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:52,430
Huh?

143
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:54,670
Hurrah!

144
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:59,120
That was a smooth move, Izzy.

145
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:00,516
Scratch that theory.

146
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:03,984
Could I have possibly
deleted the wrong program?

147
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:07,860
I think that is a distinct
possibility, Izzy.

148
00:09:08,040 --> 00:09:10,080
Why don't you try to undelete it?

149
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:11,580
Capital idea.

150
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:14,940
A stroke of metallic paint right here.

151
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:16,590
Voila!

152
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:19,937
They didn't pay their
power bill, is that it?

153
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:21,957
You know, you have no imagination.

154
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:23,511
That's rather mysterious.

155
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:25,993
And just what is so mysterious about it?

156
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:29,797
Well, you see, batteries are driven
by an acidic chemical reaction,

157
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:31,377
which generates a current.

158
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:34,519
But this one produces its
own current independently.

159
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:36,657
And what does that mean
in plain English for

160
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:38,757
those of us who didn't
major in engineering?

161
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:39,997
Give me a nano here.

162
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,551
I'll even give you three.

163
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:46,600
If I can decode this particular program,

164
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:50,317
then I can trace its base functions
and figure a way out of here.

165
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:52,640
Let's get out of here! Get closer!

166
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:59,020
Oh, this is not good.

167
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:00,740
I don't like this.

168
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:02,617
Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh.

169
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:03,759
Come on, you guys!

170
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:08,800
Lightning Blame.

171
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:14,800
And fire!

172
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:41,580
Hear me, intruders!

173
00:10:42,780 --> 00:10:44,840
Andromon will exact his vengeance

174
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,280
Hey, come on

175
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:51,377
Vengeance, we're the ones that
jump-started you, remember?

176
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:54,197
I admire the way you keep
working while your friends

177
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:58,260
are out having fun Don't you
ever feel sort of left out?

178
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:02,377
Not at all So you would rather spend
your time with puzzles than with people?

179
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:04,157
Precisely What could be
more fun than breaking

180
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:06,397
a cryptogram that nobody
else can figure out?

181
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:09,557
I can think of a lot of things like
relaxing on a nice warm beach but

182
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:12,557
you're not worrying about being
trapped in a small room with no exit.

183
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:13,758
But that's just me.

184
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:17,217
I'm just trying to determine
how we all got to this place.

185
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:19,517
And I'd like to learn
more about you, Digimon.

186
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:21,437
Perhaps prove some of my theories.

187
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:22,957
What are you theorizing about?

188
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:24,037
What's the big mystery?

189
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:25,120
I am who I am.

190
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:26,557
I'm not hiding anything.

191
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:27,470
Really?

192
00:11:27,553 --> 00:11:30,967
I simply can't understand this
preoccupation with who you are.

193
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:34,454
Is there some kind of deep,
dark secret in your origins?

194
00:11:35,740 --> 00:11:36,620
Oh, well...

195
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:41,897
don't you think it's time we
told izzy the truth dear no

196
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,117
i'm afraid if we told him now
it might be too big of a shock

197
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,157
i know you're trying to do
what's best but i worry that

198
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:51,057
once he finds out he'll be
upset we didn't tell him sooner

199
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:56,297
well we just can't think about
that now izzy izzy huh oh

200
00:11:56,380 --> 00:12:00,197
what did you say you were off
in another world look at the

201
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:05,117
screen huh it's acting rather
strangely perhaps you should

202
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:08,577
stop focusing on the problem and
start looking for a solution.

203
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:10,097
Your friends are in trouble.

204
00:12:10,180 --> 00:12:12,062
Can't you feel it in your bones?

205
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:14,310
Ah!

206
00:12:14,393 --> 00:12:16,176
And my Digivice has been activated.

207
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:18,913
Boy, that's productive.

208
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:23,062
This thing puts the pieces together,
and then it takes them apart.

209
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:31,797
Tentomon to Izzymon, are you reading me?

210
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:33,917
This is merely scientific speculations,

211
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,777
but I believe I've stumbled onto something
more than just a simple computer game,

212
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:40,742
and I'm about to abandon
my alien theory as well.

213
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:44,380
Oh, my, it's getting hot in here.

214
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:46,220
Ow, ow, I'm burning up.

215
00:12:46,380 --> 00:12:47,497
Do something quickly.

216
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:48,410
I'm being sad.

217
00:12:48,493 --> 00:12:49,660
What's going on? Hey!

218
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:50,920
Ooh, ouch.

219
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:52,580
I can't stand it. Help!

220
00:12:54,120 --> 00:12:56,100
Is it short-circuiting?

221
00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:57,470
Mercy!

222
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:01,257
I'm dancing like I've never danced before.

223
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:02,517
I'm disconnecting the power.

224
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:05,800
Now I may never figure
the mystery out. Oh, well.

225
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:07,740
Oh, I don't like computers.

226
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:10,320
What happened to my digivice?

227
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:13,760
Andromon will exact his vengeance.

228
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:15,290
Oh, yeah?

229
00:13:15,373 --> 00:13:16,611
Just try it, you tin can.

230
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:19,960
Well, come on!

231
00:13:20,420 --> 00:13:23,600
Could you please stop
taunting the deranged android?

232
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:25,670
Yeah!

233
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:31,901
Round interrupt.

234
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:34,029
Altitude reading abnormal.

235
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:36,001
Matt, I'm bored.

236
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:37,957
Gray is really my least favorite color,

237
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:40,637
and all this deconstructionism
is so ten minutes ago.

238
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:42,014
Why are we still here?

239
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:45,617
We're still here because we're trying to
figure out what gets built in this factory.

240
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:49,057
So far, it just seems designed to put
things together and then take them apart.

241
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:51,997
I really, really hope they
designed it with a door.

242
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:53,021
There's no door.

243
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:55,304
It's based on perpetual motion.

244
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:57,753
Nothing ever stops or leaves this place.

245
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:00,520
Hey, everybody!

246
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:03,311
You'll never guess what I just discovered!

247
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:06,010
Vengeance!

248
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:07,920
So what's up? Well,

249
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:10,517
the computer program operating
this factory is what's

250
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:13,424
producing the generating
power to keep it going.

251
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:16,720
Even more incredible!

252
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:21,677
In DigiWorld, basic data and simple
information are a living, viable substance.

253
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:22,590
It's alive!

254
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:24,257
Hey, you guys, listen up!

255
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:26,277
I don't like the tone of his voice.

256
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:28,337
We have got to get out of here now.

257
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:29,655
What do you mean, Tai?

258
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:34,599
Capture intruders.

259
00:14:34,820 --> 00:14:36,600
Sensors detect hostility.

260
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,540
Bring intruders into firing range.

261
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:41,230
Uh, Mr.

262
00:14:41,313 --> 00:14:42,797
Winterman, are you talking to us?

